WEBVTT

00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:03.840
Mission intake captures the request before resources are committed.

00:00:03.840 --> 00:00:07.590
It should identify the requester, purpose, decision need, location, timeframe,

00:00:07.590 --> 00:00:11.735
required data, urgency, privacy impact, deliverable, funding or authority,

00:00:11.735 --> 00:00:12.920
and known constraints.

00:00:12.920 --> 00:00:16.695
An approval workflow tests whether the mission is lawful, necessary, feasible,

00:00:16.695 --> 00:00:19.170
proportionate, and within organizational capability.

00:00:19.170 --> 00:00:22.000
Safety, aviation, site, privacy, legal, security,

00:00:22.000 --> 00:00:25.490
and data owners may review different parts according to risk.

00:00:25.490 --> 00:00:28.420
Scope defines what the crew will fly, observe, and deliver,

00:00:28.420 --> 00:00:30.180
along with explicit exclusions.

00:00:30.180 --> 00:00:33.235
A bounded scope prevents mission creep and unnecessary collection.

00:00:33.235 --> 00:00:36.995
Changes should return through a documented approval path rather than being improvised

00:00:36.995 --> 00:00:37.840
during flight.

00:00:37.840 --> 00:00:38.840
Roles must be clear.

00:00:38.840 --> 00:00:41.240
The pilot in command holds flight-safety authority.

00:00:41.240 --> 00:00:44.100
Visual observers support airspace and hazard awareness.

00:00:44.100 --> 00:00:46.120
Payload operators manage collection.

00:00:46.120 --> 00:00:47.810
Mission leads coordinate objectives.

00:00:47.810 --> 00:00:49.840
Data reviewers validate products.

00:00:49.840 --> 00:00:51.310
Maintainers control airworthiness records.

00:00:51.310 --> 00:00:56.040
Escalation paths cover rule ambiguity, weather, equipment defects, privacy concerns,

00:00:56.040 --> 00:00:58.920
emergency events, data exposure, and pressure to continue.

00:00:58.920 --> 00:01:03.330
Staff need permission to pause or reject a mission without being overruled by scheduling

00:01:03.330 --> 00:01:04.080
pressure.

00:01:04.080 --> 00:01:07.360
The operating model should define handoffs and records from intake through closure.

00:01:07.360 --> 00:01:11.640
Governance succeeds when every important decision has an owner, evidence, and review path,

00:01:11.640 --> 00:01:14.600
while the pilot retains the authority to stop unsafe flight.

00:01:14.600 --> 00:01:19.090
Set realistic service levels for intake, ordinary review, urgent escalation, scheduling,

00:01:19.090 --> 00:01:20.713
data delivery, and correction.

00:01:20.713 --> 00:01:24.590
Clear expectations prevent requesters from bypassing governance because they assume the

00:01:24.590 --> 00:01:26.200
approved process will be too slow.
